PROPOSED, NOT APPLIED — stop publishing portraits at reduced sprite tiers

    cd <repo root> && patch -p1 < dev/patches/portrait-tiers-are-never-baked-20260902.patch
    # or: git apply dev/patches/portrait-tiers-are-never-baked-20260902.patch

ⓘ THIS IS A MAIN-REPO SCRIPT, NOT THE RENDERER SUBMODULE. The brief that asked
for this expected a change in `tools/ambition_sprite2d_renderer`; the producer
is `scripts/generate_visual_quality_variants.py`, which is tracked here. So
applying it needs no submodule commit and no pointer bump — but it DOES change
generated assets on the next unfiltered regen, which is why it is a patch and
not a commit.

WHAT IT DOES. `*_portraits.png` is not a sheet page, so it falls into the
generator's `loose_srcs` bucket and `resize_png` copies it into all three
reduced tiers. **487 files / 14.2 MB that nothing can load.**
`ambition_sprite_sheet/build.rs::bake_portrait_manifests` collects portrait
manifests from `assets/sprites` ONLY, and `PortraitSheetRegistry` is built
`from_baked_table(BAKED_PORTRAIT_RONS)` and reads nothing else.

⭐ AND A CHEAPER PORTRAIT WOULD NOT BE WANTED EVEN IF IT COULD BE LOADED.
Portrait draw size is chosen by VIEWPORT, never by quality tier:
`DialogLayoutProfile::for_viewport` picks **56x62** (phone landscape), **82x94**
(phone portrait / small tablet) or **104x120** (everything else), consulting no
quality setting anywhere in that path. Against a 256x320 authored frame:

    tier              frame     @1x display          @2x display
    sprites          256x320    covers every box     covers every box
    sprites_0_5x     128x160    covers every box     smallest box only
    sprites_0_25x      64x80    smallest box only    UPSCALES ALWAYS
    sprites_potato     16x20    UPSCALES ALWAYS      UPSCALES ALWAYS

A tier under the box it is drawn into is not a cheaper portrait, it is a
blurrier one — the failure Jon's standing rule forbids. Only `0_5x` has any
case at all, and only at 1x.

⛔ ONE PREDICATE, USED AT BOTH SITES, AND THAT IS THE POINT. `prune_orphans`
deletes a variant only when its SOURCE counterpart is gone — and a reduced-tier
portrait's source is alive at full resolution, so a change that merely stopped
publishing would leave all 14.2 MB on disk forever. Sharing
`publishes_at_reduced_tier` between the publish selection and the prune means
the next unfiltered regen removes what is already there by the same rule that
stops new ones appearing.

⚠ THE FILES ARE STILL BEING PRODUCED, not left over: 439 of 475 comparable
portrait files were written in the same run as their full-resolution twin or
after it (median +3.07 days), against 44 of 44 stranded sheet pages that
PREDATE their manifest. So a clean regen elsewhere reproduces these and cannot
settle them. ⚠ mtimes are per-machine and a copy rewrites them; the evidence is
the contrast between buckets inside one tree, not the absolute dates.

▢ WHAT IS NOT INCLUDED, AND WHY. The brief also asked to stop emitting the
`gnu_ton_boss` `_full` / `_body` / `_hands` layer renders (16 files, 13.6 MB
with no manifest). They are NOT accidental intermediates: they are declared
`OUTPUT_FILES` of the gnu_ton_boss generator, and the ADR 0020 mount split
(`giant_gnu_body` = scholar-less body, `giant_gnu_hands` = hands, composited
hands-z-front) is the reason they exist. Removing them is a decision about that
mount, not cleanup, so it is recorded rather than patched.

⛔ TWO THINGS FOUND WHILE CHECKING THAT, both worth a separate look:
  * The generator's own header says *"the runtime consumes the split body/hands
    pair"*. It does not — nothing in the workspace names `gnu_ton_boss_body`,
    `_hands`, `_full` or `giant_gnu_body`, and none of them has a `.ron`, so no
    loader could reach them. A stale doc comment asserting a live contract.
  * `gnu_ton_boss_full_spritesheet.png` is byte-identical to the manifested
    `gnu_ton_boss_spritesheet.png` (same sha256, different inodes) — the
    generator says so itself, "alias for _full (back-compat)". That duplicate
    is ~5.1 MB across the four tiers and needs no ADR ruling to drop.

